<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John I Albert</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1459">1459</a>–<a href="/years/1501">1501</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Jan Olbracht · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John I Albert was a Jagiellonian king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> from 1492 to 1501, son of Casimir IV. He strengthened the rights of the nobility in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> and led a failed campaign into Moldavia in 1497. He bought the Silesian duchy of Zator in 1494 and incorporated the Masovian duchy of Płock in 1495 after its last duke died.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-i-albert.jpg" alt="John I Albert" width="320" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous (graphic from book by Zygmunt Gloger (1845-1910)); derivative work by Airam57 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PL_Gloger-Encyklopedja_staropolska_ilustrowana_T.4_454a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John Albert was born on 27 December 1459 at Wawel Castle in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, the third son among thirteen children of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> and Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. His second name honoured his maternal grandfather Albert II, in the hope of a future candidacy for the imperial throne. The chronicler Johannes Longinus (Jan Długosz) tutored him from 1467, and the Italian humanist Filippo Buonaccorsi, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callimachus">Callimachus</a>, later became his mentor and friend, urging him to limit the power of the clergy and centralise government.</p><p>The throne came to him by chance. His eldest brother Vladislaus became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1471, and the next brother Casimir died of tuberculosis in 1484. John Albert twice invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> against Vladislaus and was defeated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice">Košice</a> in 1491 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presov">Prešov</a> in 1492, but received the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glogow">Głogów</a> for life.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Casimir IV died in 1492 the Lithuanian nobles elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-jagiellon">Alexander Jagiellon</a> grand duke, breaking the personal union, while John Albert claimed the title of Supreme Duke. An electoral assembly at Piotrków proclaimed him king on 27 August 1492, helped by the intervention of his brother Cardinal Frederick, and he was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawel-hill">Wawel</a> on 23 September. In January 1493 the first two-chamber parliament, the Senate and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a>, met at Piotrków. In 1495, after Duke Janusz II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> died without heirs, he occupied Płock; a year later Konrad of Masovia paid homage and lost most of his holdings.</p><p>In 1497 he led an army of about 80,000 into Moldavia, aiming to put his brother Sigismund on the throne in place of Stephen III. The siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suceava">Suceava</a> failed between 24 September and 19 October, and the retreating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> were routed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-cosmin-forest">Battle of the Cosmin Forest</a>. He accepted a peace in 1499 that treated Stephen as an equal. Turkish raiders then reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>, and he ordered new fortifications, among them the Kraków Barbican. In 1496 townspeople were forbidden to own land and the movement of peasants was restricted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>John Albert died suddenly on 17 June 1501 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>, where he had gone to press the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> to render homage. The most likely cause was syphilis, though he suffered from other ailments and old wounds. He never married. He was buried at Wawel Cathedral on 28 July 1501, and his tomb, with an effigy by Stanisław Stwosz and a niche by Francesco Fiorentino, is regarded as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> composition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><p>Historians have judged him a valiant soldier but no commander. His reign was short and ended in a military setback, and he was criticised in his lifetime for absolutist leanings, while he is credited with creating the bicameral parliament.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John I Albert” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1484 Sultan Bayezid II captured Chilia and Cetatea Albă, cutting Moldavia off from the sea, and in 1497 Stephen beat a Polish invasion under <b>John I Albert</b> in the Cosmin Forest.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The last duke of Plock, Janusz II, died in 1495, and <b>King John I Albert</b> incorporated the duchy into the Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569).</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-plock">Duchy of Plock</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1494 Duke Jan V sold Zator to <b>King John I Albert</b> of Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-zator">Duchy of Zator</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1486" aria-label="Kraków on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John I Albert"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4450 5804h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4450 5804h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.06,19.94&amp;year=1486">What was here in 1486? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
